{
  "requirement": "Preserves the selector and a diagnostic explaining the syntax error.\n\n```rust,ignore\n#[non_exhaustive]\npub struct SelectorError {\n    pub selector: String,\n    pub message: String,\n}\n\nimpl SelectorError {\n    pub fn new(selector: &str, message: impl Into<String>) -> Self;\n}\n```\n\nTrait implementations\n\n```rust,ignore\nimpl Clone for SelectorError;\nimpl Debug for SelectorError;\nimpl Display for SelectorError;\nimpl Error for SelectorError;\nimpl PartialEq for SelectorError;\nimpl Eq for SelectorError;\n```",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The subject is the `SelectorError` struct. Its obligations are: public `selector` and `message` fields that preserve the selector and a diagnostic, `new(&str, impl Into<String>)`, and the Clone, Debug, Display, Error, PartialEq and Eq trait implementations. It is also `#[non_exhaustive]`.\n\nThe marked test is `crates/trust-but-verify/src/outline/selector.rs#mod[tests]/fn[parses_named_anonymous_and_ordinal_segments]`. It loops over 9 invalid selectors: `fn`, `fn[run`, `[run]`, `fn[run]#0`, `use@123`, `use@0123456789zz`, `use@0123456789abc`, `fn[run]x` and `a//b`. For each one it asserts:\n- `error.selector == invalid`. This covers preserving the selector and the public `selector` field.\n- `!error.message.is_empty()`. This covers the public `message` field and that a diagnostic is present.\n- `error.clone() == SelectorError::new(invalid, &error.message)`. This covers `Clone`, `PartialEq`, `new` copying both inputs into the fields, and `new` accepting `&String` through `impl Into<String>`.\n- Display, called through `&dyn std::error::Error`, contains both the selector and the message. This covers `Error` and `Display`.\n\n`assert_eq!` needs `Debug` to compile, so `Debug` is covered. `Eq` is a marker trait and the `assert_eq!` use covers `PartialEq`.\n\nThe `public_exports_match_the_appendix` test in `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/public_api.rs` checks that the type is exported at the specified path.\n\nThere is no test that two different errors compare unequal. The implementation is presumably derived, so I don't treat this as a gap. The text doesn't fix the message wording or the Display format, so asserting non-empty messages and containment is enough. I found no unacknowledged gap.",
